Think of Pizza Bolognese as the sophisticated, Italian cousin of the standard "Meat Lover’s" pizza. It’s hearty, rich, and—if done right—won't leave your crust a soggy mess.

The secret is all in the reduction. You want a ragù that is thick and concentrated, not a runny pasta sauce.

🛒 The Ingredients
The Base
Pizza Dough: 1 pound (store-bought or homemade).
Cheese: 1.5 cups low-moisture shredded mozzarella.
Finish: Grated Parmesan and fresh basil.

The Bolognese Topping
Meat: 1/2 lb ground beef (80/20) and 1/4 lb ground pork (or Italian sausage).
Aromatics: 1 small onion, 1 carrot, and 1 celery stalk (all finely minced).
Pantry: 2 tbsp tomato paste, 1/2 cup dry red wine (optional), and 1/2 cup whole milk.
Seasoning: Salt, pepper, and a pinch of dried oregano.

👨‍🍳 Step-by-Step Instructions
1. The "Extra Thick" Ragù
   Since this is going on a pizza, we need to cook out the moisture:
Sauté: In a skillet over medium heat, brown the beef and pork. Drain the excess fat, leaving just a tablespoon.
Sofrito: Add the onion, carrot, and celery. Cook until soft (about 5–7 minutes).
Concentrate: Stir in the tomato paste and cook for 2 minutes until it turns a deep brick red.
Deglaze: Pour in the wine (if using) and scrape the bottom of the pan. Once the wine has evaporated, add the milk.
Simmer: Lower the heat and simmer until the liquid is almost entirely gone. You want a "dry" meat sauce. Let it cool slightly before topping the pizza.

2. Prepare the Oven
   Preheat your oven to its highest setting—usually 475°F to 500°F (245°C to 260°C).
If you have a pizza stone, put it in while the oven preheats.

3. Assembly
   Stretch: Roll out your dough on a piece of parchment paper or a floured surface.
Layer: Spread a very thin layer of plain tomato sauce (or just a brush of olive oil) on the dough.
Toppings: Sprinkle half the mozzarella, then dollop the Bolognese sauce evenly across the surface. Top with the remaining mozzarella.
Bake: Slide the pizza into the oven. Bake for 8–12 minutes until the crust is charred and the cheese is bubbling.

💡 Pro Tips for Success
The Milk Trick: Don't skip the milk in the sauce. It tenderizes the meat and adds a silky texture that balances the high heat of the oven.
Avoid the Sog: If your sauce still looks a bit wet, strain it through a fine-mesh sieve before putting it on the dough.
The "Kick": Add a pinch of red pepper flakes to the meat while browning if you want a little heat to cut through the richness.
